    "practice": "Which time basis measures when data arrived?|Ingestion time|Business event time|The last worker restart time|The last publication time|Ingestion time tracks arrival, distinct from event occurrence and business readiness.|Occurrence time can precede arrival and measures a different freshness basis.|Worker startup is not the arrival time of an individual data record.|Publishing output occurs after arrival and measures a different stage.\nWhy enumerate expected partitions?|To expose absent partitions|To guarantee all amounts are accurate|To eliminate seasonal volume changes|To remove timezone decisions|Aggregating available data alone can conceal missing obligations.|Partition enumeration can reveal missing scope but does not establish value accuracy.|Expected scope does not remove legitimate business seasonality.|Partition expectations still need an explicit timezone and calendar.\nWhat should a volume baseline reflect?|The relevant business calendar|Only the largest historical count|Only the previous arrival interval|Only the global weekly average|Expected traffic can vary by weekday, holiday and business scope.|A high-water mark can reject legitimate low-volume calendar periods.|One prior interval may have a different business-day or holiday obligation.|A pooled mean can mask different day-specific expected volumes.\nA historical event arrives now. What can max ingestion time falsely suggest?|The business feed is fresh|Every partition is complete|All amounts are reconciled|Every key is unique|A fresh arrival timestamp does not prove recent business events are present.|A recent arrival cannot establish every required partition\u2019s presence.|Ingestion recency does not reconcile business monetary values.|Fresh arrival alone does not test key uniqueness.\nObserved volume is 80 against an expected 100. What ratio applies?|0.80|1.25|0.20|100|Observed divided by expected equals 80/100=0.80.|One point two five is expected divided by observed rather than observed divided by expected.|Zero point two is the missing fraction rather than delivered volume ratio.|One hundred is the expected count, not a ratio.\nA holiday has no contracted delivery. What should monitoring consult?|The expectation calendar|Only yesterday\u2019s count|Only today\u2019s task status|Only the highest source timestamp|Expected delivery obligations depend on the declared calendar.|Yesterday\u2019s volume may reflect a different calendar obligation.|Task success does not define whether a holiday delivery was required.|One recent source event does not establish the expected delivery calendar.\nA tenant A feed continues while tenant B stops. Which check avoids masking B?|Per-tenant expected freshness|Global maximum ingestion time only|Global maximum customer id only|Total table column count|A global aggregate can be satisfied by one active tenant while another is stale.|Tenant A can keep a global maximum fresh while B remains stale.|The largest customer id does not measure per-tenant arrival or event age.|Schema width does not establish tenant-specific freshness.",
    "formal": "Which freshness measure tracks event recency?|Latest relevant event age|Age of the latest ingestion write|Duration of the latest pipeline run|Age of the current checkpoint|Event-based freshness concerns business occurrence time rather than process uptime.|A recent arrival can contain old business events and give a misleading freshness signal.|A short successful run can still publish stale event data.|Checkpoint recency measures processing metadata rather than business event recency.\nWhat does a volume anomaly prove by itself?|A contextual volume deviation|A guaranteed fraud incident|A guaranteed schema mismatch|A guaranteed duplicate key|Unexpected count is a signal whose cause needs diagnosis.|An anomaly signals deviation without proving fraud as the cause.|Unexpected volume does not establish a schema incompatibility.|Volume can deviate without any duplicated business identities.\nWhy can a global freshness maximum mislead?|A fresh segment can mask a stale one|It always rejects fresh data|It proves full partition coverage|It removes arrival-time uncertainty|Segment-level obligations may differ even when one event is recent.|A fresh global maximum can pass while another segment remains stale.|One recent event does not prove every expected partition arrived.|A global maximum does not explain business-event versus ingestion timing.\nA Monday feed has 1000 expected rows and Saturday has 100. Which baseline fits?|Calendar-matched expectations|One universal 1000-row minimum|One universal 100-row maximum|A pooled weekly daily average|The stated business pattern requires different expected volumes by day.|A valid Saturday has only one hundred expected rows and would fail that threshold.|A valid Monday has one thousand expected rows and would fail that threshold.|A single pooled value obscures the stated day-specific business pattern.\nAn adaptive baseline declines during a three-week outage. What control helps?|Reviewed drift with contract floors|Accept every learned decline automatically|Disable missing-partition checks|Use only the final week\u2019s low values|A learning baseline can normalize persistent defects without independent obligations.|Automatic adaptation can normalize the outage as acceptable low volume.|Removing missing-partition checks further hides the outage.|Only the final low week reinforces the degraded baseline.\nAn ingestion-age alert passes but the latest event is two days old. Which action addresses the gap?|Monitor business event-time readiness|Increase the ingestion timestamp precision|Rename the timestamp field only|Reduce the number of required partitions|The two measures track different freshness concepts.|More precise arrival timestamps still measure the wrong clock for event recency.|Renaming does not change the underlying monitoring semantics.|Reducing obligations hides missing coverage instead of detecting stale events.\nA partition due at 06:00 is ready at 06:35; allowed delay is 20 minutes. How late beyond budget is it?|15 minutes|35 minutes|20 minutes|55 minutes|The permissible ready time is 06:20, so 06:35 is fifteen minutes beyond budget.|Thirty-five is total delay from due time.|Twenty is the allowance, not the excess.|Fifty-five adds delay and allowance rather than subtracting.\nVolumes 90 and 10 have baselines 100 and 10 for two segments. Which segment has the lower coverage?|The first at 90%|The second at 90%|Both at 100%|The first at 10%|The first is 90/100=90%; the second is 10/10=100%.|The second exactly meets its baseline.|The first does not meet its 100-row expectation.|Ten is its shortfall percentage, not coverage.\nA 30-minute freshness target is checked at 10:00. Latest event is 09:20 and latest ingestion is 09:58. What is the event-time result?|Missed by 10 minutes|Met with 28 minutes spare|Missed by 40 minutes|Met because ingestion is recent|The event is forty minutes old, ten beyond the thirty-minute allowance.|Twenty-eight minutes refers to the separate ingestion age allowance.|Forty is total age, not excess beyond the target.|The stated target is event-time freshness, so ingestion recency cannot satisfy it.\nA feed promises one partition per region per day. Global volume matches expectation but a region is absent and another doubles. Which gate is needed?|Region-day and volume checks|Only global volume equality|Only max ingestion timestamp|Only the largest region\u2019s count|The contract is at region-day grain and global totals can conceal offsetting omissions.|The substitution preserves the total.|A recent active region can satisfy the maximum.|The largest region does not establish coverage of every region.",
