    "practice": "Does valid JSON prove contract conformance?|No, syntax alone is insufficient|Yes, it proves every business unit|Yes, it proves required fields exist|Yes, it proves delivery timeliness|Parsing confirms syntax, while schema and semantics need separate checks.|A parser does not know business units merely because numeric syntax is valid.|Valid JSON can omit required application fields.|Parsing bytes does not prove a delivery met its deadline.\nWhich contract field explains amount interpretation?|Unit such as integer cents|Only the numeric field type|Only the serialization format|Only the source field position|Units are essential to interpreting numerical values.|An integer can represent cents, dollars or other units without a unit contract.|JSON syntax does not define a monetary amount\u2019s scale.|Position does not explain the business unit of the value.\nWhat makes a change breaking?|A consumer-assumption violation|It adds any byte to a file|It changes a comment only|It always increments a minor version|Compatibility depends on actual interface obligations and consumers.|Adding a byte can be harmless formatting rather than a breaking interface change.|Changing explanatory comments need not alter consumer-visible behavior.|A version increment does not define whether consumer assumptions are preserved.\nA numeric field switches from cents to dollars without renaming. What risk follows?|A silently changed unit scale|Automatic consumer conversion|Guaranteed parser rejection|Only different display precision|The schema can still parse while the semantic unit changes.|Consumers cannot safely infer an undocumented unit change.|Both numeric scales can parse successfully.|A hundredfold semantic amount change can survive unchanged formatting.\nA strict validator checks type(x) is int. Does True pass?|No|Yes because every Boolean is accepted|Yes because strings are coerced|Only if the key is first|Exact type comparison excludes bool even though bool subclasses int in Python.|The stated exact-type check rejects Boolean values despite Python\u2019s subclass relationship.|The stated validator does not coerce strings into integers.|Object key order does not change a value\u2019s Python type.\nA producer wants to remove a required field. What process fits?|A versioned consumer migration|Remove it and rely on retries|Rename the file only|Silence all validation alerts|Consumers must be migrated under an agreed compatibility and retirement plan.|Retries cannot restore a required field that the producer removed.|File renaming does not migrate dependent consumers.|Suppressing alerts removes detection without providing compatibility.\nA new optional field breaks a strict consumer rejecting unknown fields. What was overlooked?|Consumer compatibility rules|The producer\u2019s additive-change label|Only the field\u2019s nullable status|Only payload schema validity|Optionality does not guarantee compatibility for unknown-field-rejecting consumers.|An additive field is still incompatible when the consumer rejects unknown attributes.|Nullable values do not make an unknown attribute acceptable to a strict parser.|The producer\u2019s schema can accept an attribute that the old consumer cannot parse.",
    "formal": "What is a data contract?|A producer-consumer agreement|Only a parser-accepted schema|Only a warehouse table comment|Only a consumer\u2019s inferred format|Contracts combine structural and semantic obligations with ownership and change policy.|Syntax is one contract element; units, ownership and delivery rules also matter.|A comment alone does not establish an agreed producer-consumer obligation.|Unilateral inference is not an explicit interface agreement with the producer.\nWhich is a semantic contract change?|Cents-to-dollars unit change|Reformatting whitespace in JSON|Reordering JSON object members|Updating an explanatory comment|Units change numerical meaning even if syntax and primitive types remain valid.|Whitespace formatting does not change the amount\u2019s business interpretation.|JSON object member order does not change the amount units.|An explanatory comment alone does not change the payload\u2019s value semantics.\nWhy include an owner in a contract?|An accountable change owner|Guaranteed field accuracy|Automatic schema compatibility|Elimination of contract tests|Ownership supports coordination but does not replace validation.|An accountable owner can coordinate a defect response but does not prove each value matches reality.|Ownership does not make a changed interface compatible without consumer evidence.|Tests remain necessary even when change responsibility is clearly assigned.\nA field is present with null where a nonnull integer is required. What should validation do?|Reject under the contract|Accept because the key exists|Convert null to zero without policy|Ignore all requiredness rules|Presence and nonnull typed value are distinct requirements.|Presence is insufficient when the declared value is required to be nonnull.|Inventing zero changes business meaning without an authorized default rule.|Ignoring requiredness contradicts the stated interface contract.\nA consumer accepts only known fields. A producer adds an optional attribute. What review is required?|Review consumer unknown-field handling|Assume optional means universally safe|Remove consumer tests immediately|Increase retries on the same payload|Compatibility depends on actual decoder behavior, including unknown-field rules.|Optional at the producer does not imply strict old parsers accept unknown fields.|Removing tests discards the evidence needed to review compatibility.|Retrying unchanged incompatible payloads does not fix field-handling behavior.\nA contract version is incremented but old consumers still receive new payloads. What remains necessary?|Routing and consumer migration|Only a higher version number|Only a producer release note|Only a compressed new payload|Version labels do not isolate incompatible payloads from old consumers.|A number does not prevent old consumers receiving incompatible new payloads.|Documentation cannot route compatible payloads to the remaining old consumers.|Compression changes encoding size rather than consumer compatibility.\nAn amount of 1250 cents is interpreted as 1250 dollars. What scale error occurs?|100 times too large|10 times too large|100 times too small|No numerical error|1250 cents equals 12.50 dollars, so 1250 dollars is one hundred times larger.|Ten would apply to a decimal-place difference of one, not two.|The interpretation enlarges rather than shrinks the amount.|The unit change alters the monetary value dramatically.\nA contract requires integer cents >=0. Inputs are 0, -1, '2' and True under exact Python int typing. How many pass?|One|Two|Three|Four|Only integer zero is nonnegative and exactly int.|True is excluded by exact type policy and -1 by range.|The numeric-looking string does not meet the integer type contract.|Three independent invalid inputs are present.\nThree consumers need a field; two migrate before its removal. Which rollout state is safe under a no-breaking-change policy?|Wait for the last consumer migration|Remove it because a majority migrated|Remove it after renaming the dataset|Assume retries adapt the old consumer|All affected consumers must retain a compatible interface until their migration is complete.|Majority adoption does not protect the last consumer.|Renaming does not teach the remaining consumer the new schema.|Retries repeat the incompatible parse or semantic assumption.\nA producer changes order_id from string to integer, dropping leading zeros; consumers use it for identity. Which gate should reject the rollout?|Identity and compatibility tests|Only JSON syntax validation|Only total row-count equality|Only positive-value range checks|Changing representation can merge distinct identifiers, so key identity and consumer assumptions must be preserved.|Both forms are syntactically valid JSON.|Counts can remain equal while identity relationships change.|Positive integers can still lose leading-zero identity.",
    "problem": "Validate a strict required nonnegative integer-cents field.",
