    "practice": "Does absence from a delta prove deletion?|No|Yes for every unchanged key|Yes if the file is small|Yes if JSON parsing succeeds|Delta absence can mean no change; deletions require explicit semantics.|A delta can omit a key simply because it did not change.|Small file size is not an explicit deletion signal.|Syntax success does not establish deletion semantics.\nWhat is a tombstone here?|An explicit delete event|A field containing a null value|An expired source checkpoint|A key omitted from a delta|A tombstone carries a deletion operation for a known identity.|A null field is not necessarily an explicit entity-deletion event.|Expired progress is a recovery issue rather than a declared delete event.|An unchanged omitted key is not a deletion without explicit delta semantics.\nWhat limits replay feasibility?|Retention and durable progress|Only consumer poll frequency|Only output key sorting|Only the saved cursor value|Required history must still be available at the consumer\u2019s recovery position.|Frequent polls do not guarantee historical bytes survive a longer outage.|Sorting does not preserve source changes needed for replay.|A cursor cannot recover payloads already removed by retention.\nA daily delivery consists of four chunks and only three arrive. What should publication require?|A completeness manifest|Presence of any one chunk|A successful decompression of one chunk|A nonempty directory only|File presence does not prove the complete expected delivery set arrived.|One chunk does not satisfy a four-chunk completeness obligation.|One valid compressed chunk does not prove the other required chunks arrived.|A nonempty directory can still contain an incomplete batch.\nA revision-4 tombstone follows revision-3 update. What state follows under newest-revision policy?|Deleted at revision 4|Active at revision 3|Both active and deleted rows|Unknown because deletion is never modeled|The newer explicit deletion supersedes the older update.|Revision four is newer than the revision-three update and deletes the entity.|Newest-revision semantics select one current state rather than both simultaneously.|The scenario explicitly models deletion using tombstones.\nA correction arrives beyond the agreed lookback. What must the contract define?|An exception or repair path|Automatic silent discard always|Automatic current-day reassignment|Implicit key renaming|Late exceptions need a documented business policy rather than hidden loss.|Silent discard may violate the required correction contract and must not be assumed.|Moving a historical correction to today changes business-period meaning.|Renaming the identity does not define the correct beyond-window treatment.\nA checkpoint is saved before target persistence. What crash risk appears?|Loss of acknowledged data|Only duplicate column names|Only larger compression files|Automatic exactly-once recovery|After a crash the source may resume past changes that were never durably applied.|Premature progress can skip data; it does not imply duplicated column names.|Compression size is not the relevant progress-versus-durability boundary.|A checkpoint before target persistence does not establish atomic exactly-once recovery.",
    "formal": "What distinguishes a full snapshot from a delta?|Complete state at a stated point|A delta always lists every unchanged row|A snapshot never contains deletions|A delta has no source ordering|A full snapshot population and a change-only delta imply different absence semantics.|A delta describes changes rather than requiring every unchanged record.|A complete snapshot can express deletion by an identity\u2019s absence from that complete scope.|Change streams can and often must define ordering or revision semantics.\nWhat should a lateness contract specify?|Lateness and exception policy|Only the timestamp field type|Only the polling interval|Only the broker topic name|Consumers need both normal late-data behavior and the exception path.|Type does not define permitted delay or treatment beyond the supported window.|Polling frequency does not state which late business events are still accepted.|Topic naming does not establish lateness or historical repair obligations.\nWhat does a source position identify?|An ordered-history position|A guaranteed event occurrence time|A globally comparable wall clock|A consumer processing duration|Positions support progress and replay but may not equal event times.|An ordered source position need not encode when the business event happened.|Partitioned cursors are not automatically a global time ordering.|A source position identifies location in history rather than elapsed work time.\nA producer reuses event ids for unrelated changes. What consumer function is threatened?|Identity-based deduplication|Only stable source ordering|Only field-type parsing|Only payload decompression|Stable identity is needed to distinguish duplicates from distinct business changes.|Ordered events can still have reused identities that break consumer deduplication.|Reused ids can parse successfully while collapsing unrelated changes.|Identity reuse is a semantic problem even when payload bytes decode correctly.\nA manifest promises partitions X,Y,Z and only X,Y validate. What should happen?|Block complete publication|Declare all data complete|Invent empty Z without policy|Advance the source cursor past Z|The expected set is incomplete until Z is supplied or an approved exception applies.|A missing required partition prevents a complete-generation claim.|Inventing an empty partition changes source semantics without evidence or policy.|Advancing past missing Z can prevent recovery of its unapplied effects.\nA source retains 24 hours but the consumer outage lasts 30 hours. What is needed?|A retained source or documented gap|A guarantee replay still works|Only a shorter polling interval now|Only a new consumer name|Six hours may be unavailable, so ordinary log replay is not enough.|Thirty hours exceeds the stated retention, so complete replay is not established.|Faster polling now cannot recreate already expired source bytes.|A new consumer name cannot restore expired history.\nUpdates for one key arrive with revisions 5,3,4. Under strict newest-revision policy what revision remains?|5|3|4|12|The retained state follows the maximum revision, not arrival order or sum.|Revision three is older than the existing five.|Revision four is also older despite arriving last.|Revisions are ordering values, not additive measures.\nA source retains 72 hours and the saved cursor is 80 hours behind. What is the minimum uncovered gap?|8 hours|72 hours|80 hours|152 hours|The cursor predates retention by 80-72=8 hours.|Seventy-two is available history, not the missing portion.|Eighty includes retained history too.|Adding lag and retention does not measure the gap.\nA stream promises two-hour late corrections; the job repairs only the preceding one hour. Which design meets the contract?|Two-hour replay with keyed merge|Keep one hour and assume retries cover the rest|Accept data only by processing time|Drop all corrections as duplicates|Replay scope must cover the promised lateness and remain safe on overlapping reprocessing.|Retries do not inherently widen the scanned source range.|Processing-time reassignment changes historical event scope.|Corrections can be legitimate distinct revisions.\nA delete at revision 9 is followed by a delayed update at revision 8. The target stores only live rows and discards deletion revision metadata. What defect can occur?|Resurrection by a stale update|The deletion is guaranteed permanent|The event-time watermark must increase|The source automatically rejects the old record|Without retained version/tombstone state the consumer can mistake the stale update for a new key.|Deletion permanence needs retained ordering evidence or another explicit safeguard.|Watermark behavior is unrelated to lost per-key deletion metadata.|No source-side rejection was specified.",
